in our morning rounds we are looking out  looking at  surprise out of network medical bills  in our continuing bill of the month  partnership with kaiser health news and  npr  we examine a case where one patient says  she received an unexpected medical bill  after having knee surgery  izzy bonasso's insurance company cigna  was built  ninety six thousand dollars three  hundred and thirty ninety six thousand  three hundred thirty seven dollars by  the hospital  bonasso ended up paying 357  out of pocket for the surgery but she  also received notice on an additional  charge from an  out-of-network surgical assistant who  was part of her operation  i pivoted and  reach for a ball and i couldn't  straighten my knee after that  last summer 21 year old izzy bonasso  tore her meniscus playing tennis  she needed immediate surgery when the  hospital called  i clarified with them that the surgeon  and the hospital  and anyone would be working with me was  in network  but a month later benasso says she  learned that wasn't the case  she received two letters from eric  griffin a surgical assistant who was  in the er during her surgery griffin who  is not  in network stated he would be billing  bonasso's insurance provider  cigna he charged cigna 1167.  now this was separate from bonasso's  surgery bill  i'd never seen anything like it before  benasso's father steve responded to  griffith telling him  to work things out with the in-network  surgeon who hired him  her surgeon declined our request for  comment  he was trying to collect money from  cigna but i suspected that if he didn't  get anything from sigma he was going to  try and collect from us  cigna approved griffith's claim and  applied his fee to the bonasso's  outpatient deductible despite the  letters the bonasso's never received a  bill from griffin  or cigna i don't expect to hear from him  and i certainly don't intend to pay  i think a lot of times with medical  billing you  you know get scared that you owe  something and you pay it immediately or  at least that's how it seems i mean if i  was alone in this and without  my dad to kind of help me out i think i  probably would have seen that letter and  got scared  a spokesperson for the hospital says  quote it is a responsibility of the  surgeon  to pre-authorize the surgery and to  discuss the use of a surgical assistant  with the patient  during preoperative procedures surgical  assistants are not employed by the  hospital  the out-of-network surgical assistant  eric griffin says in a statement  that before the surgery ms benasso  signed a form acknowledging my presence  and that she was aware that i was an  out-of-network provider  the form in which ms benasso signed  including all my contact  information if she or the family had any  questions surrounding the use of an  out-of-network assistant  or my billing practices griffith says  the form also states  that the maximum she would be  responsible for is 250  and to date she has not been billed by  him for any amount  the editor-in-chief of kaiser health  news that's dr elizabeth rosenthal joins  us now to discuss  this sounds a little confusing you know  when you're in the hospital and you're  about to go in and  sign a form and then they say look you  already signed it so you knew i was  going to be there  how often does this happen it seems to  happen quite a bit  oh it happens all the time because you  know she  or in general people are presented with  this pile of forms  as you're going into surgery and what  are you going to do like you're  anticipating your surgery you're going  to read through them like legal  documents and be sherlock holmes  it's really not fair so and often the  side the these forms  are very vague they'll say you may have  an out-of-network  participant in your surgery so you know  it's just not fair to patients  so are they supposed to disclose that to  you elizabeth  ahead of time they're saying that they  did exactly that  well um again it's in a pile of forms  somewhere  and these forms are handed to you as at  your pre-op check  you know your surgeries an hour away so  i think  you know the burden should be on the  hospital and on  the doctor and i think everyone's  passing the buck here it's like oh yeah  we told her  but you know you can't expect patients  to do a major investigation when they're  doped up and nervous about their surgery  and that's what we're asking them to do  and i think the thing is here i was  going to use the idea of asking about  you know is the er doctor a network  because we know that can happen you go  to an in-network er  and there's an out-of-network doctor but  in this case this was an assistant  in the operating room she didn't even  know the person was there she's out  cold so i i think it really takes  things to a new level and puts the  responsibility on the patient in a way  that's  just not fair i know you really are very  nervous and very vulnerable at that time  it seems like there should be a better  way to do this  so what do you do if you get a surprise  medical bill like that  well i think the thing is um yes you  have to read things very carefully and  instead of signing forms you should say  will there be anyone out of network  participating in my care  in any way and if their answer is no  there won't  then then you should not pay these bills  and i think the thing is you know when  we're getting to territory  in the old days the person who helped a  surgeon would be employed by the  hospital  or it'd be a medical resident like i was  now these people are independent  contractors and they're billing  independently  and that's just not right i think the  hospital needs to take responsibility  for this  hire people hire their own surgical  assistants  or have the doctor hire a surgical  assistant who is in the same network so  patients don't get stuck  so my first check my first um you know a  piece of advice in this series is always  don't just write the check but that's  sad to have to tell people that  i know and at the time i want to know  in-network out of network i just want  somebody who will do a good job and  help me at this particular time got a  lot going on  in in those moments thank you dr  elizabeth rosenthal always good to see  you
 

